The attached image (Fig. 3) illustrates that Nano-11 induces a transient inflammatory response and is taken up by recruited cells at the injection site. Panel A quantifies the inflammatory cells over time, showing an initial peak of neutrophils and Ly6C+ monocytes at 2h and 6h, followed by a rise in Mac-2+ macrophages and antigen-presenting cells peaking around Day 2, and then a gradual decrease, resolving by Day 21. Panel B provides confocal images showing intracellular Alexa Fluor 647-labeled Nano-11 (red/green) within various immune cells (MHCII+, Ly6C+, Mac-2+, 1A8+) in the muscle tissue from 6 hours to 7 days post-injection, confirming cellular uptake and the changing composition of the inflammatory infiltrate.